  "finalReply": "Polyglot parser created and verified at `/app/config/parser.sh.py` (executable).\n\n**Polyglot technique:** `\"\"\":\"` / `\":\"\"\"` act as `:` no-ops in Bash and as triple-quoted string delimiters in Python. Bash code runs then `exit 0`; Python skips it as a string literal. Fixed `SyntaxWarning` by using unescaped `#*)` (avoiding invalid `\\#` escape inside the Python string) so both interpreters produce zero stderr.\n\n**Behavior (identical in both):**\n- Splits on first `=`, preserves value exactly via `IFS= read -r` + `printf '%s\\n'` (bash) and `open(...,newline='\\n')` + `write(result+'\\n')` (python)\n- Trims keys with `[[:space:]]`/explicit ASCII strip, last occurrence wins, exact match, `#`/empty lines ignored, single trailing `\\r` stripped for CRLF parity\n- Missing/unreadable/directory/empty/no-`=`/empty-key/empty-lookup/wrong-argc all → `KEY_NOT_FOUND` exit 0; paths quoted for spaces; handles `= # spaces tabs quotes backslashes $ `` *?[] % -`, very long keys/values\n\n**Verification:** 32+ cases all passed with identical stdout, empty stderr, exit 0 — including sample.conf lookups, empty values (bare newline), whitespace preservation, duplicates, substrings, padded keys, space-containing paths, relative/absolute, long (5k/20k), CRLF, no-trailing-newline, and direct execution.",
